  {:el :requirement
   :id :sbom-tool.requirement/stable-exit-codes
   :name "stable, distinct exit codes"
   :desc "The process exit code tells success, policy violation, runtime error and usage error apart."
   :doc "### Scenario

A CI job decides from the exit code whether to fail the build, and whether the failure is a policy violation or a broken run.

### Measure

Exit code 0 is success, 1 a policy violation, 2 a runtime or data error and 3 a CLI usage error or failed --validate (adapter.ui.cli). These codes are a public contract. Inferred from the code, not stated as a requirement.

### Quality attribute

Reliability and operability."
   :tags #{"quality"}}

  {:el :requirement
   :id :sbom-tool.requirement/actionable-diagnostics
   :name "actionable error messages and diagnostics"
   :desc "Errors name the failing input and the cause; --debug gives details for diagnosis, and stdout stays clean for reports."
   :doc "### Scenario

A user passes a malformed SBOM or policy file, or a lookup fails.

### Measure

Errors are typed and carry a friendly message instead of a stack trace. Diagnostics go to stderr, so report output on stdout can be piped. Inferred from the code, not stated as a requirement.

### Quality attribute

Usability and analysability."
   :tags #{"quality"}}

  {:el :requirement
   :id :sbom-tool.requirement/predictable-report-order
   :name "predictable report ordering"
   :desc "Reports list vulnerabilities by severity and license counts in a defined order."
   :doc "### Scenario

A user compares reports or reads the most important findings first.

### Measure

Vulnerabilities per component are sorted by severity, most severe first (application.report). The license summary is sorted by count, then name (adapter.report.markdown). Other lists are not known to be sorted, so full determinism of all output is not claimed. Inferred from the code.

### Quality attribute

Usability and predictability."
   :tags #{"quality"}}

  {:el :requirement
   :id :sbom-tool.requirement/bounded-lookup-load
   :name "bounded load on external services"
   :desc "Live lookups use batching and a fixed number of concurrent requests."
   :doc "### Scenario

A large SBOM is looked up on OSV, deps.dev or the GitHub Advisory Database.

### Measure

The number of concurrent requests is bounded per adapter (fixed thread pool) and OSV requests are chunked. No time budget or concurrency target is defined, and the defaults are partly guesses (see risk external-service-limits). Inferred from the code.

### Quality attribute

Performance efficiency and resource use."
   :tags #{"quality"}}

  {:el :requirement
   :id :sbom-tool.requirement/pure-domain-core
   :name "pure, I/O-free domain core"
   :desc "Domain and application code know neither SBOM formats nor I/O, and dependencies point inward."
   :doc "### Scenario

A developer changes parsing, scoring or policy logic.

### Measure

The domain layer has no dependency on application or adapter code, and adapters parse into the canonical SBOM model. This can be checked by the code model's :dependency relations. Source: AGENTS.md (architecture rules).

### Quality attribute

Maintainability and testability."
   :tags #{"quality"}}

  {:el :requirement
   :id :sbom-tool.requirement/fault-tolerant-lookups
   :name "lookup failures do not abort the run"
   :desc "A failed live lookup is reported as a warning and the remaining results are still produced."
   :doc "### Scenario

A vulnerability service times out or returns an error for some components.

### Measure

The run completes with the data available. A failed chunk or id is dropped with a warning on stderr. The downside is recorded in the risk failed-lookup-looks-like-clean-result. Inferred from the code.

### Quality attribute

Reliability (fault tolerance)."
   :tags #{"quality"}}

